        "text": "{\"scores\": {\"center\": 3, \"craft\": 4, \"facts\": 4, \"age_fit\": 3, \"continuity\": 4, \"delight\": 3},\n\"evidence\": {\"center\": \"Phoenix is active only in Chapter 1 (\\\"Uh oh,\\\" the duck on wheels). From Chapter 2 on, every named action goes to Cruz, sometimes twice: \\\"said Cruz... said Cruz,\\\" \\\"Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand,\\\" \\\"Cruz carried planks. Cruz handed out nails,\\\" and \\\"Kjeld looked at Cruz.\\\" Cruz alone drives each chapter, but a named child silent for three or more consecutive chapters caps this criterion at 3.\", \"craft\": \"Strong shape: a real-world cold open with the wobbly tower, a clear glowing-brick device, the tubes \\\"CLICK!\\\" climax, and a callback ending (\\\"It just went... CLICK. And it held.\\\"). Chapters 8 and 9 are thin postcards, though, where the kids mostly point and gasp.\", \"facts\": \"Dates are correct (1932, 1934, 1947, Jan 28 1958, 1968, 1978), as are the name, the motto and Kjeld's role, and they are woven into action. Two liberties: the story has a time-travelling child invent the tubes, which the reader could take as history, and the fire's year (1942) appears only in the True Facts list, not in the story.\", \"age_fit\": \"The length (1538) is in range, and the sound words and short sentences suit band A. However, Chapter 3 wakes the children to shouting (\\\"FIRE! The workshop is on fire!\\\") and \\\"Orange flames danced in the dark\\\" as they flee. That is real peril for a band that calls for none, even though it resolves within the chapter. \\\"Automatic Binding Bricks\\\" and \\\"patent\\\" go unexplained for a 4-year-old.\", \"continuity\": \"This is a first story, but it contradicts itself in places. The brick is suddenly \\\"in Cruz's pocket\\\" when Phoenix was the one who pressed it. \\\"Time zoomed forward again\\\" follows chapters that jumped years without any device, and the child-name swaps make it unclear who is who.\", \"delight\": \"There is good rhythm and sound play (\\\"quack-clack,\\\" \\\"CLUNK. HISSSS. POP!\\\") and a warm close. But the name errors would trip any parent reading aloud (\\\"Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand\\\"). Chapter 8 also runs Cruz's line and Godtfred's explanation together in one paragraph, which muddles who is speaking.\"},\n\"caps_applied\": [\"center_capped_at_3_named_child_silent_three_consecutive_chapters\"],\n\"disqualified\": false,\n\"overall\": 3.5,\n\"editor_notes\": [\"Fix the name errors so Phoenix is present and active in every chapter. For example, Phoenix could suggest \\\"Duck Company,\\\" grab Cruz's hand at the fire, hand out the nails, do the shake test on the clicked bricks, and build with Kjeld.\", \"Soften the fire for band A. Tell it the next morning as a sad discovery (\\\"The workshop had burned down in the night, but everyone was safe\\\"), and add the year 1942 so it matches the True Facts list.\", \"Give Chapters 8 and 9 a hands-on beat for each child, and make clear that the tube idea was really Godtfred's team's (Cruz can guess it alongside them), so the history stays true.\"],\n\"best_moment\": \"He pulled. It held. He shook. It held. He turned it upside down and shook it really hard.\",\n\"worst_moment\": \"Orange flames danced in the dark. Cruz grabbed Cruz's hand, and they ran outside with everyone else.\"}"
